  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1158 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nBefore 11am, heat indices in southeastern Minnesota have already \nclimbed into the upper 90s thanks to dew points pushing 80. Further \nwest, it's quite a bit cooler following last night's thunderstorms. \nSome cloud cover is also playing into this, but clouds will continue \nto clear and allow temperatures to warm quickly into the mid to \nupper 90s by early afternoon. We'll have no trouble seeing apparent \ntemperatures top 100 area wide with readings near or above 110 \npossible across southern Minnesota. These temperatures and dew \npoints will contribute to a very unstable airmass with CAPE values \ntopping 3000 J/kg. However, models continue to show 700mb temps \nsomewhere around 13 to 14C which would help to limit convection. The \nmain focus for severe weather later today will likely lie across \nWisconsin, where damaging winds and large hail will be the primary \nthreat. As mentioned in the previous discussion, if 700mb temps do \nend up being a degree or two cooler, we could see things evolve \nfurther west across Minnesota. CAMs have pretty consistently been \nshowing storms firing in the Arrowhead and tracking into northern \nWisconsin as an MCS this evening, so confidence remains highest for \nour easternmost Wisconsin counties. SPC did introduce a large \nenhanced risk (level 3/5) that includes pretty much all of Wisconsin \nwith the slight risk area still extending back into western \nMinnesota. Did a morning PoP update to raise precip chances (10-20%) \nCWA wide to keep messaging with SPC more consistent.\n\nHeading into Monday, highs will be in the 90s again, but we'll see \nwinds switch around to the northwest, allowing some drier air to \nwork into the region. This will help tamp down heat indices a \nlittle, but readings in the mid to upper 90s are still possible \nacross our south. Went ahead and expanded the Heat Advisory from \njust the Twin Cities metro to include southern Minnesota and \nportions of western Wisconsin. By Wednesday, the axis of the 500mb \nridge will extend up through the Dakotas. A shortwave along the top \nof this ridge will move across the eastern Dakotas/Minnesota \nsometime late Wednesday/early Thursday, which brings in our next \nround of precip chances. The timing of this feature will impact \nwhether or not we see strong to severe storms on Thursday. Looking \nahead, some low precip chances remain in place with highs in the \nupper 80s to start the month of August.",
